---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Cell from '../../components/arch/Cell.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';

// Side view of the drum: centre (CX, CY), radius R, window = 2 * arc with arc = 1 rad.
const R = 130;
const CX = 450;
const CY = 210;
const onArc = (phi: number) => [CX - R * Math.cos(phi), CY + R * Math.sin(phi)] as const;
const ticks = [-1, -1 / 3, 1 / 3, 1].map(onArc);

// Three mini boards for the focus modes, three reels each.
const focusModes: [string, number, string][] = [
  ["'reel'", 0, 'weight 0: own centreline'],
  ["'set-lean'", 0.5, 'weight 0.5: halfway'],
  ["'set'", 1, 'weight 1: board centre'],
];
---
<Architecture
  title="Curvature"
  subtitle="How a flat strip is projected onto a drum, in two modes."
  eyebrow="Drum"
>
  <p>
    A real slot reel is a cylinder. The cell facing you is full size; the ones
    above and below have rotated away, so their far edges sit further from
    your eye and a camera draws them as trapezoids, not smaller rectangles.
    pixi-reels fakes that with one model per reel,
    <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelCurve.ts</code>, and draws it in one
    of two ways: per symbol, through a perspective mesh, or per reel, by
    bending a render texture. The motion layer keeps laying the strip out flat;
    the curve is a pure function from a flat coordinate to a quad.
  </p>

  <h2>The drum</h2>

  <Diagram height={400} caption="Side view. The window's half extent is wrapped onto a cylinder; the middle cell (phi = 0) is drawn 1:1 and everything else is pushed through a perspective divide that shrinks it by how far it has receded. On the right, the keystone you see: far edges narrower, edge cells shorter along the strip; the buffers past the window continue on a straight line.">
    <Label x={64} y={42} text="flat strip" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => (
      <Cell x={40} y={70 + i * 54} size={48} label={['buf', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'buf'][i]} kind={i === 0 || i === 4 ? 'buffer' : 'plain'} />
    ))}
    <rect x={34} y={118} width={60} height={162} rx="8" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 4 3" />

    <Label x={CX - 40} y={42} text="side view" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Box x={130} y={188} w={90} h={44} kind="ghost" title="camera" mono={false} />
    <path d={`M ${ticks[0][0]} ${ticks[0][1]} A ${R} ${R} 0 0 0 ${ticks[3][0]} ${ticks[3][1]}`} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-width: 2" />
    <line x1={ticks[0][0]} y1={ticks[0][1]} x2={ticks[0][0] + 22} y2={ticks[0][1] - 34} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    <line x1={ticks[3][0]} y1={ticks[3][1]} x2={ticks[3][0] + 22} y2={ticks[3][1] + 34} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    {ticks.map(([x, y]) => <circle cx={x} cy={y} r="4" class="arch-marker-primary" />)}
    <circle cx={CX} cy={CY} r="3.5" class="arch-marker-muted" />
    <line x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={CX - R} y2={CY} class="guide" />
    <line x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={ticks[3][0]} y2={ticks[3][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    <line x1={220} y1={210} x2={ticks[0][0]} y2={ticks[0][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 2 4" />
    <line x1={220} y1={210} x2={ticks[3][0]} y2={ticks[3][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 2 4" />
    <Label x={CX - R + 14} y={CY + 22} text="phi = 0" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={ticks[3][0] + 16} y={ticks[3][1] + 12} text="phi = -arc" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={CX - R + 14} y={CY - 16} text="r = halfExtent / arc" size={14} mono tone="fg" />

    <Label x={630} y={42} text="what you see" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <g class="cell cell-buffer"><polygon points="608,70 652,70 654,116 606,116" class="cell-bg" /></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="606,120 654,120 660,172 600,172" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="151" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">A</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="600,176 660,176 660,240 600,240" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="213" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">B</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="600,244 660,244 654,296 606,296" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="275" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">C</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-buffer"><polygon points="606,300 654,300 652,346 608,346" class="cell-bg" /></g>
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The numbers behind the picture:
    <code>halfExtent = (visibleCells * pitch - (pitch - cellMain)) / 2</code>,
    <code>arc = amount * MAX_ARC</code> (1.0 rad, about 57 degrees),
    <code>k = depth / (1 - cos arc)</code>,
    <code>scale(phi) = 1 / (1 + k * (1 - cos phi))</code>, which is 1 at the
    middle and <code>1 / (1 + depth)</code> at the window edge, and
    <code>main(phi) = h + h * sin(phi) * scale(phi) / arc</code> inside the
    window, a straight line past it.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'visible cell', note: 'projected inside the window' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'buffer cell', note: 'fills the band the drum leaves short of the window edge' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'ReelCurve', note: 'pure projection, no positions written' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'renderer', note: 'PerspectiveCell or ReelWarp' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>ReelCurveConfig</code> is <code>{`{ amount, depth? }`}</code>;
    <code>builder.curve(0.35)</code> and <code>curve({`{ amount: 0.35 }`})</code>
    mean the same thing, and <code>curvePerReel([...])</code> sets one per reel.
    <code>resolveCurveConfig</code> clamps <code>amount</code> to 0..1, defaults
    <code>depth</code> to <code>amount * 0.5</code>, and clamps depth below
    <code>0.9 * cos(arc)</code>: past <code>cos(arc)</code> the projection
    stops being monotonic and cells at the window edge fold back over each
    other. <code>depth</code> is defined as the shrink at the window edge, so
    <code>1 / (1 + k(1 - cos arc)) = 1 / (1 + depth)</code> fixes
    <code>k</code>. The middle is normalised to 1:1 (<code>norm = arc</code>):
    pinning the ends to the window instead would stretch the middle row along
    the main axis but not across it, which reads as a squashed centre. So the
    drum's ends fall short of the window edges, and the buffer cells fill that
    band. Past the window, position continues as a straight line from where
    the edge actually landed (<code>_edgeSlope</code>) while scale keeps
    following <code>1 - cos(phi)</code>, so a buffer cell is never a flat
    rectangle beside a hard-curved neighbour. An arc below
    <code>1e-4</code> reports <code>isFlat</code> and the builder does not
    create a curve object at all, which keeps a flat set byte-identical to an
    uncurved build.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>setGeometry(cellMain, cellCross, pitch, visibleCells)</code> binds
    the projection to real sizes; <code>Reel.setCurve</code> derives
    <code>cellMain</code> from <code>motion.slotPitch - mainGap</code> so a
    MultiWays reel is measured on the strip it is actually spinning on, and
    <code>Reel.reshape</code> re-binds it. <code>quadFor(mainStart, inset)</code>
    projects the rectangle the art really occupies: for a trimmed atlas frame
    <code>cellInset</code> (from <code>textureCellInset</code>) is much smaller
    than the cell, and projecting the whole cell would inflate the symbol and
    give it the cell's keystone instead of its own, milder one. The quad is
    returned <strong>view-local</strong>, clockwise from the screen's top-left,
    so art stays upright on a horizontal set. <code>mapMain(main)</code> and
    <code>scaleAt(main)</code> expose the same projection for overlays.
  </p>

  <h2>The invariant: the curve never writes a position</h2>

  <p>
    <code>view[mainProp]</code> is load-bearing: <code>Reel</code> reads it back
    in <code>beginMotion</code>, <code>notifyLanded</code> and
    <code>_replaceSymbol</code> to recover which slot a view is in, and a bent
    value taken for a flat one compounds on every round trip. So the curve is
    handed over as a quad and <code>ReelSymbol.applyCellQuad</code> must never
    move <code>view.position</code>; the base implementation pays for its fit
    entirely through the pivot. <code>ReelSet.getCellBounds(reel, cell)</code>
    measures through the same map and returns the quad's axis-aligned bounding
    box (a payline through its centre tracks the curve; an outline on it is a
    hair loose on the narrow edge). <code>getCellQuad(reel, cell)</code> returns
    the four corners in <code>ReelSet</code> space, or <code>null</code> on a
    flat reel. Use it for hit areas in warp mode and for anything that should
    sit on the curve rather than around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to draw it</h2>

  <Diagram height={410} caption="curveMode('symbol'), the default. Each cell is projected on its own and costs nothing extra: the flat position is written first, the curve hands the symbol a quad, and a texture that owns its whole source is keystoned through a mesh while anything else falls back to an affine fit.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={60} title="ReelMotion._render()" sub="flat main written first, as always" />
    <Box x={180} y={104} w={360} h={60} title="curve.quadFor(main, inset)" sub="view-local quad, null when flat" />
    <Box x={180} y={188} w={360} h={60} kind="accent" title="symbol.applyCellQuad(quad)" sub="SpriteSymbol, AnimatedSpriteSymbol" />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 164]} to={[360, 188]} />
    <Box x={60} y={308} w={260} h={78} kind="primary" title="PerspectiveCell" sub={['PerspectiveMesh 10 x 10', 'setCorners(quad), own UVs']} />
    <Box x={400} y={308} w={260} h={78} kind="muted" title="affine fallback" sub={['atlas sub-frames, Spine, Graphics', 'contain-fit via pivot, no bend']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 248]} via={[[360, 278], [190, 278]]} to={[190, 308]} label="whole-source texture" labelDx={-12} />
    <Edge from={[360, 248]} via={[[360, 278], [530, 278]]} to={[530, 308]} label="otherwise" />
  </Diagram>

  <Diagram height={500} caption="curveMode('warp'), which needs builder.renderer(app.renderer). The whole reel is rendered to a texture every tick and the texture is bent, so everything inside follows at one extra render pass per reel per frame; the symbols themselves are never handed a quad.">
    <Box x={160} y={20} w={400} h={60} title="reel.container leaves the stage" sub="ReelWarp takes its place in maskedContainer" />
    <Box x={160} y={104} w={400} h={78} title="RenderTexture" sub={['reel box + one slotPitch per strip end', '+ curveBleed per side across it']} />
    <Box x={160} y={206} w={400} h={60} kind="accent" title="renderer.render(container)" sub="every tick, even at rest" />
    <Box x={160} y={290} w={400} h={78} kind="primary" title="MeshPlane 16 x 16" sub={['vertices displaced once per geometry', 'cross toward focus, main through mapMain']} />
    <Box x={160} y={392} w={400} h={78} kind="ghost" title="symbols untouched" sub={['no quads handed out, no double bend', 'lifted content draws flat above the drum']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 182]} to={[360, 206]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 266]} to={[360, 290]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 368]} to={[360, 392]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    <strong>Symbol mode</strong> is crisp and free, but a <code>Container</code>
    transform is affine: it can scale and move a cell, never turn it into a
    trapezoid. Only content that <em>is</em> a texture keystones.
    <code>PerspectiveCell</code> (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/symbols/PerspectiveCell.ts</code>)
    is shared by <code>SpriteSymbol</code> and <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol</code>;
    it allocates its <code>PerspectiveMesh</code> lazily, re-reads the texture
    on every <code>apply</code> so an animated symbol can swap frames,
    rewrites the mesh UVs from <code>texture.uvs</code> on each texture swap
    (a mesh's 0..1 UVs address the whole sheet, a sprite's do not), and
    pivots the mesh on the quad's centre so a win pulse scales in place.
    <code>canProjectTexture</code> accepts only textures that own their whole
    source: generated textures, single images, render textures. An atlas
    sub-frame, the common production case, falls back to the base class's
    affine contain-fit, correct but not keystoned. <code>SpriteSymbol.onActivate</code>
    calls <code>perspective.syncTexture</code> so a pooled swap keeps the
    quad; <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol</code> pushes every frame change through.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Warp mode</strong> (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelWarp.ts</code>)
    bends whatever is inside the reel: Spine skeletons, atlas sprites, text,
    effects, with no cooperation from the symbol, and it sidesteps the
    sub-frame problem because a render texture owns its whole source. The
    texture is the reel box grown by one <code>slotPitch</code> at each end of
    the strip (the drum's ends curve away from the window and the buffer cells
    fill that band) and by <code>curveBleed</code> per side across it, for art
    wider than its cell. It is created at the renderer's resolution. Each
    tick the source container is moved to the texture's origin plus that
    margin, rendered with <code>clear: true</code>, and moved back in the same
    synchronous block; this runs even at rest because win pulses, cascade
    destroys and the spotlight animate symbols on a resting reel and the
    texture is all the player sees. The mesh's vertices are displaced once per
    geometry change, not per frame, and <code>resize</code> re-measures after a
    MultiWays reshape. The cost is one extra render pass per reel per frame and
    one resample, so hairline art is marginally softer. Symbols are no longer
    real display objects to the renderer, so use <code>getCellQuad</code>
    rather than screen-space hit tests. Anything the engine <em>lifts</em> out
    of the reel container, <code>unmask: true</code> symbols at rest, the win
    spotlight, pin overlays, is not in the texture and draws flat above the
    drum; the builder says so with the <code>warp-skips-unmask</code> notice.
    <code>build()</code> throws if warp mode is chosen without
    <code>renderer(...)</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the camera sits</h2>

  <Diagram height={300} caption="curveFocus moves the cross coordinate each reel's cells converge on, three reels shown. At weight 0 it is the reel's own centreline; at 1 it is the middle of the board, so the outer reels lean in and the grid reads as one drum.">
    {focusModes.map(([name, w, note], m) => {
      const gx = 12 + m * 236;
      const gw = 224;
      const y0 = 60;
      const boardCentre = gx + gw / 2;
      return (
        <>
          <Group x={gx} y={20} w={gw} h={250} kind="solid" label={`curveFocus(${name})`} />
          {[-1, 0, 1].map((r) => {
            const colCentre = boardCentre + r * 56;
            const focus = colCentre + w * (boardCentre - colCentre);
            const lean = (focus - colCentre) * 0.35;
            return (
              <>
                <g class="cell cell-buffer"><rect x={colCentre - 18 + lean} y={y0} width={36} height={44} rx="6" class="cell-bg" /></g>
                <Cell x={colCentre - 22} y={y0 + 52} size={44} kind="plain" />
                <g class="cell cell-buffer"><rect x={colCentre - 18 + lean} y={y0 + 104} width={36} height={44} rx="6" class="cell-bg" /></g>
                <line x1={colCentre + lean} y1={y0 - 8} x2={colCentre} y2={y0 + 74} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
                <line x1={colCentre} y1={y0 + 74} x2={colCentre + lean} y2={y0 + 156} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
              </>
            );
          })}
          <line x1={boardCentre} y1={y0 - 12} x2={boardCentre} y2={y0 + 160} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 1.5 4" />
          <Label x={boardCentre} y={y0 + 190} text={note} anchor="middle" />
        </>
      );
    })}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Per reel, <code>focusCross = cellCross / 2 + weight * (setCentre - reelIndex * (cellCross + crossGap) - cellCross / 2)</code>,
    reel-local, with <code>CURVE_FOCUS_WEIGHT = {`{ reel: 0, 'set-lean': 0.5, set: 1 }`}</code>.
    Under <code>'reel'</code> the per-reel <code>RectMaskStrategy</code> is
    fine; the other two auto-select <code>SharedRectMaskStrategy</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Leaning cells cross their own column, and the default per-reel
    <code>RectMaskStrategy</code> would clip them at the boundary, so any focus
    other than <code>'reel'</code> switches the default mask to
    <code>SharedRectMaskStrategy</code> and logs the <code>mask-auto-shared</code>
    notice; an explicit <code>.maskStrategy(...)</code> always wins. The focus
    only has an effect alongside <code>curve(...)</code> or
    <code>curvePerReel(...)</code>. In warp mode the same focus feeds the
    vertex displacement, so the whole texture leans. The curve can be swapped
    at runtime: <code>ReelSet.setCurve(input)</code> drives
    <code>Reel.setCurve</code> on every reel, and <code>ReelMotion.setCurve</code>
    flattens every symbol with <code>applyCellQuad(null)</code> before dropping
    to <code>amount: 0</code>, otherwise the last quad it handed out would stay
    on screen. The <a href="/recipes/reel-curvature/">reel curvature recipes</a>
    show both modes, the focus settings and a payline drawn on the drum; the
    mask side is on <a href="/architecture/viewport-and-masks/">viewport and masks</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Defaults</h2>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Item</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Value</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Where</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ['amount', '0 (flat); clamped to 0..1', 'core/ReelCurve.ts'],
          ['depth', 'amount * 0.5, then min(depth, 0.9 * cos(amount * MAX_ARC))', 'core/ReelCurve.ts'],
          ['MAX_ARC / MIN_ARC / DEPTH_SAFETY', '1.0 rad / 1e-4 / 0.9', 'core/ReelCurve.ts'],
          ['curveFocus', "'reel' (weights reel 0, set-lean 0.5, set 1)", 'core/ReelSetBuilder.ts'],
          ['curveMode', "'symbol'", 'core/ReelSetBuilder.ts'],
          ['curveBleed', '0 px per side, warp only', 'core/ReelSetBuilder.ts'],
          ['PerspectiveCell VERTICES', '10 x 10', 'symbols/PerspectiveCell.ts'],
          ['ReelWarp GRID', '16 x 16 vertices', 'core/ReelWarp.ts'],
          ['Warp texture margin', 'slotPitch at each strip end, renderer.resolution', 'core/Reel.ts, core/ReelWarp.ts'],
          ['Mask when focus != reel', 'SharedRectMaskStrategy (auto, unless explicit)', 'core/ReelSetBuilder.ts'],
        ].map(([k, v, w]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs">{k}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-primary">{v}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{w}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Where this lives</h2>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mb-1">packages/pixi-reels/src/core/</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>ReelCurve.ts</code> ReelCurveConfig, resolveCurveConfig, CurveFocus, CurveMode, CURVE_FOCUS_WEIGHT, quadFor, mapMain, scaleAt</li>
        <li><code>ReelWarp.ts</code> the render-to-texture mesh, update, resize, _displace</li>
        <li><code>Reel.ts</code> _buildCurve, setCurve, _placeSymbolView, the warp hand-off in the constructor</li>
        <li><code>ReelMotion.ts</code> setCurve, quadFor in _render</li>
        <li><code>ReelSet.ts</code> getCellBounds, getCellQuad, setCurve</li>
        <li><code>ReelSetBuilder.ts</code> curve, curvePerReel, curveFocus, curveMode, curveBleed, renderer, the mask auto-pick</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mb-1">packages/pixi-reels/src/symbols/</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>PerspectiveCell.ts</code> textureCellInset, canProjectTexture, PerspectiveCell</li>
        <li><code>ReelSymbol.ts</code> cellInset, applyCellQuad (affine fallback)</li>
        <li><code>SpriteSymbol.ts</code>, <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol.ts</code> the two built-ins that project</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mt-4 mb-1">config/types.ts</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>ReelCellQuad</code> (box plus x0..y3), <code>ReelCellInset</code> (left, top, right, bottom as fractions)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Architecture>
